    "lawSection": "Public Health",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S8290\n\nSPONSOR: MAY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to authorize and direct the commissioner of health to establish a\nportable diagnostics program\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to enhance accessibility to screening\nservices for Medicaid members, especially the elderly and disabled,\nthrough the use of mobile medical imaging technologies, commonly known\nas portable diagnostics.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nThe bill creates a new program that would expand and make permanent\ncoverage of portable diagnostic services for Medicaid members enrolled\nin fee-for-service and Medicaid managed care. Rates of reimbursement and\nsetting locations would mirror those of the Medicare program.\n\nSpecifically, the commissioner of health is authorized and directed to:\n  *establish a portable diagnostics program that demonstrates the cost-\neffectiveness of medical assistance coverage of portable diagnostic\nservices, including X-rays, electrocardiograms, echocardiograms and\nultrasounds, provided to medical assistance recipients enrolled in both\nfee-for-service Medicaid and Medicaid managed care. Such services shall\ninclude those allowable and or provided by portable diagnostic providers\nas authorized by title 42 of the code of federal regulations.\n  *reimburse portable diagnostics at the Medicare fee schedule by\ncurrent procedural terminology codes, transportation codes, and set-up\ncodes. Such rates of reimbursement shall apply to Medicaid recipients\nenrolled in fee-for-service Medicaid, Medicaid managed care, and Medi-\ncaid managed long-term care; and\n  *report to the legislature by December 31, 2024 on the cost-effective-\nness of the program. The report shall include a cost comparison of port-\nable diagnostics, including X-rays, electrocardiograms, and ultrasounds,\nprovided in an authorized portable diagnostic setting compared to the\nsame procedure in a hospital, urgent care and emergency room setting.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nPortable diagnostics are cost-effective and offer safety and convenience\nto both patients and providers by bringing mobile imaging directly to a\nperson, and avoiding costly hospitalizations. For example, effective use\nof telemedicine, including tele-radiology in older adults, resulted in a\n34% decrease in emergency room visits. The most common mobile imaging\nservices currently offered are X-ray, ultrasound, electrocardiograms,\nand echocardiograms.\n\nThis program created by the bill will increase access to and utilization\nof portable diagnostics for the frail, elderly and disabled. Specif-\nically, this bill will increase the Medicaid fee-schedule to mirror\nMedicare, including all CPT codes for portable x-ray, ultrasound, echo-\ncardiogram and EKG, including the Q-Code for setup and an\nupstate/downstate blend for R-Code for transportation.  Additionally,\nthe bill expands settings to mirror Medicare, mandates health plans\n(MCOs, MLTC) reimburse in accordance with Medicare, and recognizes that\nthe AMA & CMS set Medicare rates based on cost. As a result, there will\nbe lower costs to the Medicaid program through reductions in the unnec-\nessary use of urgent care visits, emergency room visits, and hospital\nstays.\n\nAdditionally, as the state continues to face the challenges of the\nSARS-CoV-2 (Covid) pandemic, increasing the use of mobile imaging\nservices greatly reduces the risk of hospital-borne Covid infections for\nthe most vulnerable patients served.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nSignificant savings to the state. Estimates range from $1 million to $10\nmillion depending on the tests diverted from hospitals\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately\n",
